bin/
obj/
.vs/
*.user

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RazorGraph code graphs -- AND A WARNING, because these patterns are broad.
#
# WHY: a code graph is volatile derived data. It represents source this
# repository already contains, it is wrong the moment anything is edited, and
# git keeps every stale copy forever once one is committed. Same category as
# obj\. There is no version worth storing -- only a note of when one was taken
# and by what tool, which belongs in the document that cites it.
#
# WARNING: "graphs" and ".graph.json" are ordinary names, and these rules do
# not know the difference between a generated code graph and real content you
# authored. If you keep chart data, diagrams, plotting output or anything else
# under a graphs\ directory or ending in .graph.json, GIT WILL IGNORE IT
# SILENTLY -- it will simply never appear in git status, and nothing will tell
# you why. That is this rule doing its job, not a bug.
#
# If a file you expected is missing from git status, ask git which line is
# responsible:
#
#     git check-ignore -v path\to\your\file
#
# To keep something that matches anyway, either force it in once with
#     git add -f path\to\your\file
# or, better, add a negation BELOW these lines so it survives the next clone:
#     !docs/diagrams/*.graph.json
#
# Files already tracked are unaffected: gitignore only governs untracked ones.
#
# solution-graph.json is named explicitly because it is the default output of
# RazorGraph.Cli build-solution, so it lands in whatever directory the tool
# was run from rather than anywhere deliberate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.graph.json
solution-graph.json
graphs/
.graph/
